  "title": "Understanding the Cognitive Immersion of Hospitality Architecture in Culture and Nature: Cultural Psychology and Neuroscience Views",
  "authors": "Haihui Xie et al., 2022",
  "central_proposition": "The aesthetic appreciation of hospitality architecture, especially when immersed in culture and nature, is mediated by the interplay of sensory-motor, knowledge-meaning, and emotion valuation systems, leading to cognitive rejuvenation.",
  "concept_definitions": [
    {
      "name": "Aesthetic Triad",
      "definition": "A general neural model for architectural aesthetic experience, comprising sensory-motor, knowledge-meaning, and emotion valuation systems, derived from Vitruvian's firmness, usefulness, and aesthetic virtue."
    },
    {
      "name": "Sensory-Motor Neural System",
      "definition": "The system responsible for initial architectural contact through vision (luminance, color, motion) and non-visual senses (odor, sound, temperature), involving parahippocampal, occipital, retrosplenial cortex, and motor responses (nucleus accumbens, anterior insula) for approach/avoidance."
    },
    {
      "name": "Knowledge-Meaning System",
      "definition": "The system linking architectural appreciation to memories, past experiences, cultural settings, cognitive maps (hippocampus), familiarity, expectations, context, and cultural background, which shape aesthetic knowledge and meaning."
    },
    {
      "name": "Emotion Valuation System",
      "definition": "A pivotal system that mediates and regulates feelings and emotions generated by architectural aesthetics, balancing them to avoid excesses, and is improved by exposure to nature."
    },
    {
      "name": "Fluency (architectural)",
      "definition": "The spatial and visual ease of processing architectural elements, demonstrated by vertical alignment, translucent materials, and reflections."
    },
    {
      "name": "Complexity (architectural)",
      "definition": "The harmonious unity of multiple design levels or methods within a building, exhibiting a logical hierarchy (macroscopic, middlescopic, microscopic)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Naturalistic Patterns (architectural)",
      "definition": "Organic design elements in buildings that directly, indirectly, or symbolically elicit human affinity for natural features and processes, conferring psychological benefits similar to interacting with nature."
    },
    {
      "name": "Cognitive Rejuvenation",
      "definition": "The restoration of cognitive functions, such as directed attention and memory, through exposure to natural environments, as explained by Attention Restoration Theory (ART)."
    }
  ],
  "argument_structure": "The paper first establishes the theoretical foundation of the aesthetic triad (sensory-motor, knowledge-meaning, and emotion valuation systems) based on Vitruvian's principles and neuroaesthetic literature. It then develops three propositions, one for each system, detailing how architectural features (fluency, complexity, naturalistic patterns, familiarity, expectations, context, cultural meaning) interact with these systems. Finally, it uses a single case study (Amanfayun Hotel) to provide empirical support for these propositions, concluding with a discussion on the central roles of culture and nature in cognitive rejuvenation.",
  "mechanism_or_causal_logic": "The paper proposes that architectural aesthetic appreciation is a multi-system process. The sensory-motor system processes basic visual (luminance, color, motion) and non-visual (odor, sound, temperature) cues, leading to initial approach/avoidance responses via the nucleus accumbens and anterior insula. This processing is influenced by the knowledge-meaning system, which integrates past experiences, cultural background, and expectations, engaging hippocampal cognitive maps to build familiarity and assign meaning. These two systems' interactions are then moderated by the emotion valuation system, which, mediated by the brain's reward circuitry and improved by nature exposure, regulates and balances the overall emotional response to architecture. This integrated process, particularly when architecture incorporates cultural and natural elements, leads to cognitive rejuvenation by restoring directed attention and reducing stress.",
